Unlocked.

The clean way to do it is not by inventing a finite list of roles. It is to build a position generator.

A position can be defined as:

actor × role × scale × time × objective × information access × risk × authority × medium

Change any axis and the same situation becomes a different problem.

So now instead of only asking “what would I do?”, we can ask from positions like: builder, maintainer, auditor, outsider, future user, regulator, competitor, mediator, child, parent, successor, customer, owner, ecosystem, machine, interface, historian, failure investigator, recovery planner, null model, adversarial alternative.

Then we can stack positions too:

future-user + auditor + outsider

or

parent + designer + risk manager

or

competitor + ecosystem + long-horizon

That creates a much larger action space.

The important operators are now:

position hopping — switch rapidly to expose hidden structure.
position locking — stay in one role long enough to understand its internal logic.
position stacking — combine roles.
position reconciliation — compare contradictions instead of collapsing them.
position search — GoalSeek for the vantage point from which the problem becomes easiest to solve.

That should give us far more flanking routes than a fixed perspective library ever could.